WHAT IS LIST?
A group of heterogeneous and indexed collection of mutable elements whose insertion order is maintained and can be represented as a whole using [] bracket can be termed as a list in python. A list in python allows duplicate values because unlike set there is index and can be used to differentiate between two similar elements.
Example L1 = []
L1.append (3) -> insert at the end of the list
L1.insert (2, 3)
L1 [0:4]
L1 [3:]
L1 [:5]
** note that slicing can happen only from left to right and not from right to left.
L1 [-1:-4]  error
L1 [-4:-1]
L1.pop() – removes the last element and returns the element
L1.pop(2) – removes the element at index 2 and returns the element
l1.sort() – ascending order
l1.sort(reverse = True) – descending order

WHAT IS A TUPLE?
Tuple is similar to List in every possible ways but it is immutable in nature. Once a tuple is created, we cannot remove, add or modify the elements of a tuple. The elements can only be accessed using index. 

t1 = (3 , "Rashmi" , -4)
type(t1)
t1.count(3) – number of times this element appears in the tuple
t1.index('Rashmi') – index of this element


It is also used as key in a dictionary or object in python.
# Creating a dictionary with tuples as keys
student_data = {
    ("Alice", 25): {"major": "Computer Science", "GPA": 3.8},
    ("Bob", 22): {"major": "Mathematics", "GPA": 3.5},
    ("Charlie", 23): {"major": "Physics", "GPA": 3.7}
}

# Accessing values using tuple keys
print (student_data[("Alice", 25)]["GPA"])  # Output: 3.8

WHAT IS SET?
Set is an unordered, heterogeneous and mutable collection of elements; which does not allow any duplicates and is represented using {} bracket. It means that a set can only contain unique elements.
Ex - S1 = {}
Type(s1)
S1.add(21)
S1.add(“Rashmi”)
S1.pop()
S1.remove(21)

SET OPERATION – union, intersection and difference
Union: A ∪ B. Union will return a new set which contains all the elements from both A and B. 
A.union(B) or A | B
Example:
A = {1, 2, 3}
B = {3, 4, 5}
A ∪ B = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}
Intersection: A ∩ B. This operation creates a new set which contains only the common elements from both the set A and B.
A.intersection(B) or A & B
Example:
A = {1, 2, 3}
B = {3, 4, 5}
A ∪ B = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}

Difference: A – B. This operation creates a new set which contains the elements of A which are not present in set B. 
A.difference(B) or A - B
Example:
A = {1, 2, 3}
B = {3, 4, 5}
A - B = {1, 2}

WHAT IS A FROZENSET?
Similar to tuple, a frozenset is a special kind off set which is immutable in nature. Once a frozenset is created, its elements cannot be removed, modified or added. Since it is immutable in nature, it can be used for hashing as keys in a dictionary.

WHAT IS A DICTIONARY?
A collection of key value pairs, separated by commas can be termed as a dictionary
D1 = {1 : “A” , 2 : “B”}
D1.keys() – returns a list of keys
D1.items() – returns a list of tuple of the key-value pair
D1.values() – returns a list of the values 
D1.popitem() – returns the last element and remove it

len Method – This method takes in an iterable as an input and returns the length of that iterable object.
len([1,2,3,4]) – 4

input method – Takes input from the user and return it as a string
a = input() -> whatever the user provide will be returned a string
b = input(“insert a number”) -> “quoted message for the user to understand”

TYPE CASTING – Type casting is allowed in python when it is logically feasible. i.e there should be relevance between the data type we are trying to convert.
For example – 
S1 = ‘12’
V1 =int(S1) ---> no problem because it a string that holds int literal
S2 =’rashmi’
V2 =int(S2) ---> error! Because it is not possible to convert this string to base 10 integer
L1 =[1,3,5]
T1 =tuple(L1)
L2 = [“rashmi , “rashmi” , 4, 5, 7,9,9,0]
S2 = set(l2)  --  {“rashmi” , 4 , 5 ,7,9,0}  ---> no problem, it will convert to a set and the duplicate values will be removed
i1 = int(input("insert a number"))  --->  input method and type-casting together. Directly converting to int while taking input from user.
i2 = int(input("enter a number")) enter a number rashmi 21 ---> error! Because if we put anything other than int literal it cannot convert to base 10 int value; so an error is encountered.
